  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nHighly amplified flow pattern continues over the CONUS with midlevel \nridging re-building over the upper Great Lakes in the wake of \nyesterday's shortwave, with 500 mb heights around the 95th \npercentile of climo by 12Z this morning. Meanwhile a deep, expansive \ntrough is centered near the northern CA/NV border, with strong SW \nflow between these features. Closer to home, an area of stratus over \nroughly the northern half of the UP is keeping temps very mild in \nthe mid-40s to near 50 as of 07Z, while clearing and decoupling has \nallowed temps to fall into the mid-30s over southern portions.\n\nToday, ridging extending from an upper Ohio Valley high in the \nmorning will give way to an increasing pressure gradient as low \npressure organizes in the lee of the Rockies. This low will rather \nquickly move northeastward today into tonight in concert with a lead \nwave ejecting from the parent trough; this low will take a very \nsimilar track to the one from yesterday from the northern Plains to \nJames Bay. This will set up another period of gusty winds from late \nthis evening through early Saturday morning. Extent of gusts is \ntricky here since it's a warm advection scenario overnight, but some \nforecast soundings maintain a mixed layer for at least part of the \nnight in locations like Ironwood, Herman, and Marquette. Current \nexpectation is for 30-35 mph gusts for the western and northern UP, \nup to 45 mph at times for higher terrain and areas immediately \ndownwind. As for temps, another unseasonably warm day is expected \nwith mid-50s to low 60s for highs. Overnight lows tonight will be \nridiculously warm for Nov. 21 in the upper 40s to mid-50s (see \nclimate section below for records). \n\nThe aforementioned low will gradually drag a cold front across the \nUP Saturday evening to early Sunday. Northward moisture transport \nwill interact with the frontal circulation, drawing in a plume of \n0.8-0.9 inch PWATs. However, showers Saturday should remain rather \npaltry until late afternoon in the west but especially Saturday \nnight when forcing for ascent due to DCVA increases and spins up a \nlow along the front. Due to the forcing and slow movement of the \nfront, this should be a fairly efficient rainfall producer in a \nsomewhat narrow plume whose location is still uncertain. Latest NBM \nprobs are 30-60% exceeding an inch, highest over \nAlger/Delta/Schoolcraft. Near record high temps Saturday in the \nupper 50s to mid-60s fall Saturday night behind the front and \ncontinue to stay steady or fall through the day Sunday as the \nsteadier precip tapers off from west to east. \n\nLake effect precipitation kicks off Sunday night into Monday with \n850 mb temps gradually falling into the minus single digits Sunday \nnight and bottoming out around -12C Monday. A potentially \ninteresting interaction with a trailing wave and surface trough with \na low-level convergence signature could focus a heavier band of \nrain/snow across a portion of the western UP/Keweenaw/north-central \nSunday night, although it would have to overcome marginal \nthermodynamics. Otherwise, lake effect snow in the north to \nnorthwest wind belts is forecast to result in light accumulation \nthrough Monday (30-50% exceeding 2\" and 10-20% exceeding 4\" in the \nheaviest bands). When temps become more favorable, moisture \ndecreases, which will limit accumulations. Temperatures finally \nreturn to seasonal normals with highs in the 30s and lows in the \nteens Monday/Tuesday, with high pressure ending lake effect precip \nMonday night. An early look toward the Thanksgiving holiday favors \ndry weather and seasonable temps with high pressure lingering.",
